{"records":[{"id":"dby7kdtzk8","title":"Cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-Positive Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified: A systematic review and comparative analysis with Epstein-Barr Virus-negative, leg type","description":"Supplement 1. PRISMA flow diagram of literature search and study selection. \nEBV+ DLBCL, NOS, Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified; PRISMA, Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses.\n\nSupplement 2. Histopathological features of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified. (A) Pleomorphic atypical large cells in a background rich in reactive cells such as lymphohistiocytes and plasma cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). (B) Monotonous sheets of immunoblast-like large cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). Atypical large lymphoid cells of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified highlighted by (C) MUM-1 (x200), (D) BCL-2 (x200), and (E) CD30 (x200); Epstein-Barr Virus infection detected by (F) positive Epstein-Barr encoding region in situ hybridization (x200).\n\nSupplement 3. Survival analysis of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified (EBV+ DLBCL, NOS). (A) Difference in overall survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and primary cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-negative diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type (EBV- DLBCL, leg type). (B) Comparison in survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and concurrent cutaneous and extracutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-27T07:21:33.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dby7kdtzk8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dby7kdtzk8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Woo Jin ","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma","Lymphoma of the Skin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tfg83w73v3","title":"Epstein-Barr Virus Induced One-Carbon Metabolism Drives B Cell Transformation","description":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) causes Burkitt, Hodgkin, and post-transplant B-cell lymphomas. How EBV remodels metabolic pathways to support rapid B-cell outgrowth remains largely unknown. To gain insights, primary human B-cells were profiled by tandem-mass-tag based proteomics at rest and at 9 time points after infection. >8000 host and 29 viral proteins were quantified, revealing mitochondrial remodeling and induction of one-carbon (1C) metabolism. EBV-encoded EBNA2 and its target MYC were required for upregulation of the central mitochondrial 1C enzyme MTHFD2, which played key roles in EBV-driven B-cell growth and survival. MTHFD2 was critical for maintaining elevated NADPH levels in infected cells, and oxidation of mitochondrial NADPH diminished B-cell proliferation. Tracing studies underscored contributions of 1C to nucleotide synthesis, NADPH production and redox defense. EBV upregulated import and synthesis of serine to augment 1C flux. Our results highlight EBV-induced 1C as a potential therapeutic target and provide a new paradigm for viral onco-metabolism.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-28T09:48:57.435Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tfg83w73v3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tfg83w73v3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolism","Epstein-Barr Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y395v54y8k","title":"GPCR signaling contributes to immune characteristics of microenvironment and process of EBV-induced lymphomagenesis","description":"Natural killer T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous lymphoid neoplasm of natural killer or cytotoxic T cells in association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. In the present study, we performed a comprehensive proteogenomic characterization of 112 NKTCL, incorporating genomics, transcriptomics, and deep-scale proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-22T07:19:27.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y395v54y8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y395v54y8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvsrtknwh5","title":"Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","description":"This dataset contains the supplementary materials for manuscript entitled: A Database-Driven In Silico Approach to Identify Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-02T15:30:59.401Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvsrtknwh5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvsrtknwh5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Norazlin","last_name":"Abdul Aziz","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Host-Pathogen Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7gwtb7mgrr","title":"Genomic and Transcriptomic Characterization of NKTCL","description":"Natural-killer/T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous entity of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. To identify molecular subtypes of NKTCL based on genomic structural alterations and EBV sequences, we performed multi-omics study on 128 biopsy samples of newly diagnosed NKTCL and defined three prominent subtypes.\n\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-02T03:32:10.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7gwtb7mgrr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7gwtb7mgrr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s5d8fkzwhj","title":"A Study of Viral-Mediated AURKB Cleavage Promotes Cell  Segregation and Tumorigenesis. Zhu Q et al","description":"Aurora B (AURKB) kinase, a central regulator of chromosome segregation and cytokinesis, is aberrantly expressed in various cancer cells. However, the relationship of AURKB and oncogenic viruses in cancer progression remains unclear. Here, we reveal that N-cleaved isoforms of AURKB exist in several oncovirus-associated tumor cells and patient cancer tissues, including Kaposi’s sarcoma herpesvirus (KSHV), Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), and human papillomavirus virus (HPV). Mechanistically, in KSHV-infected tumor cells，the latent viral antigen LANA cleaves AURKB Asp76 in a serine protease-dependent manner. The N’-cleaved AURKB relocalizes to the spindle pole and promotes the metaphase-to-telophase transition in mitotic cells. Introduction of N’-AURKB but not C’-AURKB promotes colony formation and malignant growth of tumor cells in vitro and in vivo using a murine xenograft model. Altogether, our findings uncover a proteolytic cleavage mechanism by which oncoviruses induce cancer cell segregation and tumorigenesis.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-25T15:02:35.698Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s5d8fkzwhj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s5d8fkzwhj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qing","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenguo","last_name":"Zi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shujun","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuyan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caixia","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenghong","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fang","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiliang","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncovirus"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":20177,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":39,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":20177,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":20115,"restricted":13,"embargoed":49},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":249},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":102},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","count":82},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":77},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":77},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":61},{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","count":61},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":58},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":53}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":19737},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":244},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":83},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":35},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":20},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":17},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":10},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":10},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":6},{"id":"ea6ba14d-86b5-44fc-8476-80e67f7fc1af","name":"Demo Uni","count":5}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":82},{"id":"e9576611-ab17-4985-9129-33439e6b617a","name":"IRCCS Ospedale San Raffaele","count":81},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":64},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":63},{"id":"aa1a9d6a-538c-5f58-b674-31c2823b1acc","name":"Stanford University","count":63}]}}